<ResourceDictionary xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
                    xmlns:media="clr-namespace:System.Windows.Media;assembly=PresentationCore">

    <!--
        WHAT A CELL IS AND WHAT IT SHOWS. The cell itself, the five ways a word can be
        written in one, and the templates the meaningful columns are drawn with. Not one
        value of its own - every number, colour and thickness below is a name declared in
        Values.xaml.

        THE MARKS LEFT ON 2026-08-25 AND THE SENTENCE THEY BREAK IS FURTHER DOWN THIS
        COMMENT, REWRITTEN RATHER THAN LEFT TO ROT. They are in Marks.xaml, which is merged
        immediately before this file - the templates below name StatusMark, StartMark and
        AgainstMark through StaticResource, which resolves while this file is being read.

        ADR-23, the same rule as the other three files: a value invented here would be as
        invisible as one invented in a view, and the guard treats all four alike.

        SPLIT OUT OF List.xaml ON 2026-08-12, AND ONE THING WAS REWRITTEN IN THE SAME STEP -
        said plainly, because the previous split's comment says "nothing rewritten" and
        copying that sentence here would have made it a lie. Everything below moved unchanged
        EXCEPT the two mark styles, which gained a shared base and lost an inheritance.

        That repair is why the split happened at all. List.xaml stood exactly on the 507-line
        markup ceiling, and `StartMark` could not be fixed without adding lines to it: it was
        BasedOn `StatusMark`, so it inherited triggers reading the RUN STATE, and an entry
        that was running wore a green fill in the column about its NEXT start. Backlog 166.
        A ceiling that forces a seam is the ceiling working.

        WHY THE SEAM IS HERE. List.xaml keeps what the list is BUILT FROM - the grid, the
        row and the template it owns, the headings. This keeps what a person actually READS
        in a cell: its words, and what a cell is made of.

        THE SENTENCE THAT USED TO CLOSE THAT PARAGRAPH IS NO LONGER TRUE AND IS REPLACED
        RATHER THAN DELETED. It said "everything about a cell is on this side, which is what
        makes the name answerable without opening the file", and on 2026-08-25 the marks
        moved out. The name is still answerable and the test is the same one every half of
        this theme has had to pass: this file holds the WORDS in a cell, Marks.xaml holds the
        coloured shapes beside three of them. What was bought with that is written at the head
        of Marks.xaml, and what it cost is this paragraph.

        NONE OF THEM LOADS ON ITS OWN. This one reaches WPF UI's keys -
        DefaultDataGridCellStyle, BodyTextBlockStyle - and ours in Values.xaml, and the three
        mark styles in Marks.xaml, and StaticResource resolves all of them WHILE THE FILE IS
        BEING READ. So the merge order in App.xaml is the library's dictionaries, then
        Values.xaml, then Controls.xaml, then List.xaml, then Marks.xaml, then this. Anything
        that loads these files elsewhere has to do the same, which is why WpfHost and
        tools/wpfui-probe both build the stack in that order.

        LAST OF OURS, AND THAT IS A DIRECTION RATHER THAN AN ACCIDENT. An implicit style
        merged later wins, so the most specialised half sits on top. Nothing here is implicit
        today, so the order costs nothing now and is the one that will still be right the day
        something is.
    -->

    <!--
        The cell carries spacing and, since 2026-08-24, its own template. It paints nothing at
        all: what fills a row is the row - see the block about it in List.xaml.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="ListCell" TargetType="DataGridCell" BasedOn="{StaticResource DefaultDataGridCellStyle}">
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="{StaticResource PadListCell}" />
        <!--
            Against their MinHeight of 32. Row height is decided by the padding above, which is
            the same decision as the margins on the row: keep the density this list had.
        -->
        <Setter Property="MinHeight" Value="{StaticResource SizeNone}" />

        <!--
            THE CELL OWNS ITS TEMPLATE SINCE 2026-08-24, THE LAST PLACE THIS LIST BORROWED ONE.
            Theirs wraps the ContentPresenter in a Border that paints a fully transparent brush and
            binds nothing else - one element per cell of every realised row, to be invisible.

            BasedOn IS KEPT, unlike what the row did in 2026-08-05: the row left because their
            TRIGGERS fought ours, and this one has none - read with tools/wpfui-probe, not assumed.
            It still carries KeyboardNavigation.IsTabStop=False, which the row's focus ring needs.
            Stretch is load-bearing too, because a TextBlock sized to its own text never reaches the
            width where CharacterEllipsis would trim it. AND IT TAKES NO MOUSE HITS - their border
            was what the pointer landed on in the padding, 106 of 603 sampled points, so List.xaml
            now supplies one transparent surface per row instead of one per cell. Backlog 221.
        -->
        <Setter Property="Template">
            <Setter.Value>
                <ControlTemplate TargetType="DataGridCell">
                    <ContentPresenter Margin="{TemplateBinding Padding}"
                                      HorizontalAlignment="{TemplateBinding HorizontalContentAlignment}"
                                      VerticalAlignment="{TemplateBinding VerticalContentAlignment}"
                                      SnapsToDevicePixels="True" />
                </ControlTemplate>
            </Setter.Value>
        </Setter>
    </Style>

    <!--
        WHAT EVERY CELL IN THE LIST IS MADE OF, AND THE TRIMMING IS THE POINT.

        Without it a cell too narrow for its text cuts it wherever the pixels run out, halfway
        through a character, with nothing to say it did - which reads as a rendering fault
        rather than as "this does not fit". Measured on the real window: a display name wanted
        476 pixels in a column of 365, and at 1039 pixels of window there is no width at which
        the longest of them fits. Something has to give and the person has to be able to see
        that it did.

        The tooltip is the other half of the same rule. Text that gives way has to be gettable -
        and ONLY such text, since 2026-08-25. Worked out when asked rather than bound on every
        cell of every row, which is what the switch below turns on. `CellTips` has the argument.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="CellText" TargetType="TextBlock" BasedOn="{StaticResource BodyTextBlockStyle}">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFace}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource TextSizeNormal}" />
        <Setter Property="TextTrimming" Value="CharacterEllipsis" />
        <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center" />
        <Setter Property="ToolTip" Value=" " />
    </Style>

    <!--
        THE ONE COLUMN THAT IS PROSE RATHER THAN A VALUE. IT GREW FOR A CHOSEN ROW FOR ONE DAY AND
        THE OWNER REVERTED IT BY EYE - see the note under this comment. Backlog 192, built on
        2026-09-01 out of three roads and taken out the same day by the only reader who can judge
        how it looks. What follows is why it was built, kept because the measurement is still worth
        having and the next attempt should not have to make it again.

        WHAT THE COMPLAINT WAS. A description is up to 1251 characters on this machine and the cell
        gave it one line with an ellipsis, so the column showed the first few words of a sentence
        and nothing else. Two answers already stood and both were partial: Enter opens the details
        panel, which shows the whole thing wrapped, and the context menu copies it.

        THE NUMBERS THAT DECIDED THE CAP, measured 2026-09-01 over the 800 entries of this machine
        rather than argued: 448 of them carry a description at all, the median is 95 characters, the
        ninetieth is 321 and the longest is 1251. So a row that grew to fit EVERY description would
        be thirty lines tall for one entry in a hundred, and a cap of four lines shows the whole of
        a description for most of the list while leaving the tail to the panel that already handles
        it. Four is where the reading stops being a peek and starts being the text.

        ONLY WHILE THE ROW IS CHOSEN. A list of 810 rows is read by scanning down a column, and rows
        of different heights are what makes that scanning stop working - which is the argument that
        kept this unbuilt until somebody decided. So the list at rest is exactly what it was, and
        one row at a time is allowed to be tall.

        THE COST, SAID RATHER THAN LEFT OUT. This is one binding per description cell, walking one
        level from the TextBlock to the cell it sits in. The row template lost a FindAncestor
        trigger on 2026-08-31 for costing exactly this kind of thing - bindings per row went from 8
        to 7 - so this puts one back, on ONE column, and only while that column is switched on. A
        property trigger with no binding cannot do this job: a cell style cannot reach the text
        inside the cell, and TextWrapping is not an inherited property, so nothing travels down.

        THE LINE BOX IS SET HERE RATHER THAN INHERITED, so that the cap really is four lines. WPF has
        no TextBlock.MaxLines - that property belongs to WinUI - so the only cap available is a
        height, and a height is only four lines if something says how tall a line is. The trimming
        comes down from CellText untouched, so the fourth line still ends with an ellipsis when
        there is more.

        Vertical alignment is left centred, which is a choice rather than an oversight: the other
        cells of a tall row sit in the middle of it. Top-aligning them would mean touching every
        templated cell in this file for a row state none of them otherwise knows about.
    -->
    <!--
        AND THE OWNER LOOKED AT IT ON 2026-09-01 AND SAID NO. Everything above this line is the
        argument for a chosen row that grows, the measurement behind the four line cap, and the
        reasoning about scanning a column - all of it still true, and none of it the reason this
        was reverted. It looked bad on the screen. That is a verdict this project has no arithmetic
        for and the owner is the only reader who can give it.

        THE KEY STAYS, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE. ListColumns names it for the description column, and
        a name that means "this cell is prose rather than a value" is worth keeping even when it
        currently says nothing more than CellText does - the column is still the one that holds
        sentences, and the next thing done to it belongs here.

        WHAT REMAINS FOR THE COMPLAINT THIS TRIED TO ANSWER: Enter opens the details panel, which
        shows the whole description wrapped, and the context menu copies it. Both were already
        there and both still are. Backlog 192 goes back to being open.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="CellProse" TargetType="TextBlock" BasedOn="{StaticResource CellText}" />

    <!--
        Numbers line up on their last digit, text on its first. Complaint 3 of the eleven, and
        it is how a column of process ids becomes comparable at a glance instead of being read
        one row at a time.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="CellNumber" TargetType="TextBlock" BasedOn="{StaticResource CellText}">
        <Setter Property="HorizontalAlignment" Value="Right" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        MONOSPACED, AND ONLY WHERE ALIGNMENT CARRIES MEANING - a launch path and a security
        descriptor. `docs/03` part 4 names those two and a hash: reading a hash in a proportional
        face is how two different hashes come to look the same, and the same is true of two paths
        differing by one character deep inside them.

        Everything else about it is CellText, including the trimming, which matters more here
        than anywhere: a descriptor is several hundred characters and will never fit.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="CellFixed" TargetType="TextBlock" BasedOn="{StaticResource CellText}">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFaceFixed}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        WHAT A ROW STANDS FOR BESIDES ITSELF - the count `A11` asks for beside a folded template,
        "CDPUserSvc - 4 instancje".

        SUBDUED, BECAUSE IT IS NOT THE NAME. The cell beside it is the identity a plan is built
        from, and a count in the same weight would compete with it down a column of 800 rows.
        TextSubdued rather than a new brush: ContrastGuards already holds it to the text threshold,
        so this cannot be the eleventh brush nobody checked.

        COLLAPSED WHEN THERE IS NOTHING TO SAY, rather than drawn empty. Almost every row on the
        machine stands only for itself - 775 of 798 measured here - so a badge that merely had no
        text would still take part in layout and push every name in the list sideways for the
        benefit of twenty-three of them.

        The trimming comes down from CellText and is left alone deliberately: this column is Auto,
        so the badge never gives way, and `CellTips` refuses to show a tooltip over text that did
        not - so the placeholder it inherits costs a decision at hover time and nothing at all per
        row.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="CellBadge" TargetType="TextBlock" BasedOn="{StaticResource CellText}">
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextSubdued}" />
        <Setter Property="Margin" Value="{StaticResource MarginBetweenControls}" />

        <Style.Triggers>
            <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding StandsFor}" Value="">
                <Setter Property="Visibility" Value="Collapsed" />
            </DataTrigger>
        </Style.Triggers>
    </Style>

    <!--
        THE TWO CELLS THAT CARRY A MARK AS WELL AS A WORD, AND THEY MOVED HERE FROM THE MIDDLE OF
        MainWindow.xaml ON 2026-08-11. `docs/11` 3.1 asks for shape AND colour AND label and calls
        any two of the three insufficient, which is why these two columns are templated at all.

        <b>They are here rather than in the view because the columns are built in code now.</b>
        A template written inline inside a column definition has no name, so nothing can ask for
        it - and S6d2 turns seventeen columns into a list somebody chooses from, which means the
        columns are constructed rather than declared. A resource dictionary is the one place a
        column built in code can reach.

        THE MARK AND THE WORD SIT IN A GRID, NOT A StackPanel, and that is load-bearing: a
        horizontal StackPanel measures its children against infinite width, so the text would
        never learn it has to give way and the ellipsis would never appear. The trimming would be
        present, correct and dead - which is the shape of fault this window has shipped four times.

        THE WORD COMES THROUGH THE INDEXER AND THE MARK THROUGH A PROPERTY, and that is not an
        inconsistency. The word is a cell, so it is asked for by the column's identifier like
        every other cell in the list. The mark is not a cell - it sits beside one - and it is a
        code rather than a word precisely so that a trigger never compares against translated text.
    -->
    <!--
        THE NAME, AND WHAT THE ROW STANDS FOR BESIDES ITSELF - `A11`.

        THE WORD COMES THROUGH THE INDEXER AND THE BADGE THROUGH A PROPERTY, which is the same split
        the two cells below use and it is not an inconsistency. The name is a cell, so it is asked
        for by the column's identifier like every other cell in the list. The badge is not - it sits
        beside one - and keeping it out of the cell is what stops it reaching the clipboard, the
        sort and the plan, all three of which read the identity.

        THE NAME TAKES THE STAR AND THE BADGE IS Auto, so the badge lines up down the right edge of
        the column and the name is the half that gives way. A count that moved with the length of
        each name would have to be read row by row, which is complaint 3 of the eleven in `docs/11`
        arriving at a different column.

        A Grid rather than a StackPanel, for the reason spelled out over the cells below: a
        horizontal StackPanel measures its children against infinite width, so the name would never
        learn it has to give way and its ellipsis would be present, correct and dead.
    -->
    <DataTemplate x:Key="NameCell">
        <Grid>
            <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                <ColumnDefinition Width="*" />
                <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" />
            </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
            <TextBlock Grid.Column="0"
                       Text="{Binding [serviceName]}"
                       Style="{StaticResource CellText}" />
            <TextBlock Grid.Column="1"
                       Text="{Binding StandsFor}"
                       Style="{StaticResource CellBadge}" />
        </Grid>
    </DataTemplate>

    <DataTemplate x:Key="StatusCell">
        <Grid>
            <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" />
                <ColumnDefinition Width="*" />
            </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
            <Ellipse Grid.Column="0" Style="{StaticResource StatusMark}" />
            <TextBlock Grid.Column="1"
                       Text="{Binding [status]}"
                       Style="{StaticResource CellText}" />
        </Grid>
    </DataTemplate>

    <DataTemplate x:Key="StartTypeCell">
        <Grid>
            <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" />
                <ColumnDefinition Width="*" />
            </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
            <Ellipse Grid.Column="0" Style="{StaticResource StartMark}" />
            <TextBlock Grid.Column="1"
                       Text="{Binding [startType]}"
                       Style="{StaticResource CellText}" />
        </Grid>
    </DataTemplate>

    <!--
        The third one, same shape of cell as the two above - mark, then word, in a Grid rather than
        a StackPanel for the reason spelled out over them.
    -->
    <DataTemplate x:Key="AgainstStartTypeCell">
        <Grid>
            <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" />
                <ColumnDefinition Width="*" />
            </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
            <Path Grid.Column="0" Style="{StaticResource AgainstMark}" />
            <TextBlock Grid.Column="1"
                       Text="{Binding [runsAgainstItsStartType]}"
                       Style="{StaticResource CellText}" />
        </Grid>
    </DataTemplate>
</ResourceDictionary>
